WEBVTT


00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.009
Knitting away in tuck.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.980
It's getting late and oops, I dropped a
bunch of stitches when trying to shape an

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:08.670
arm hole.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:15.990
Now for me, picking up in tuck is
nearly impossible in desperation.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.200
Determined not to have to start all over.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:25.440
I tried four different techniques to pick
up the last row of plain knitting before I

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.460
started tuck.

00:00:27.345 --> 00:00:30.765
First I picked up a small
circular hand knitting needle.

00:00:31.515 --> 00:00:33.915
Now, this is normally my go-to method,

00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:41.685
but I was only able to find a bamboo
needle and it was really clumsy to pick up

00:00:41.685 --> 00:00:43.455
the stitches in this ribbon yard.

00:00:45.614 --> 00:00:50.775
Next, I used a double eye needle to
run a strand through the stitches.

00:00:52.815 --> 00:00:54.615
But that felt like driving a truck.

00:00:54.645 --> 00:00:56.655
It was just too thick and bulky.

00:00:58.245 --> 00:01:03.765
So I use the same idea of running a strand
of yarn through the stitches, and I found

00:01:03.765 --> 00:01:06.045
a tapestry needle to work much better.

00:01:15.045 --> 00:01:17.385
Finally, I had the brilliant idea.

00:01:17.774 --> 00:01:21.945
That using a fine wire from the
ribber cast on comb would work.

00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:23.595
Yep.

00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:29.955
It was fine enough to pick up the
stitches, but you may have already guessed

00:01:29.955 --> 00:01:31.905
the flaw in this idea.

00:01:34.905 --> 00:01:35.475
Okay.

00:01:35.505 --> 00:01:39.735
I was sure that all the stitches were
picked up in one way or another, and I

00:01:39.735 --> 00:01:44.625
carefully ripped out all the tuck
now to rehang all those stitches.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:51.450
Rehanging the stitches from the hand
knitting needle would have been a whole

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:56.910
lot easier if I had taken time to
find a smaller metal circular needle.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:03.880
rehanging the stitches from the
pink strand of yarn wasn't too bad.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:24.630
But there was a closed end on the cast on
comb wire that I used, I had to transfer

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:29.820
all of those stitches from the wire to
the hand knitting needle, then rehang the

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.200
stitches on the needle bed.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:33.510
Oh my gosh.

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:36.870
A blocking wire would have
been a much better choice.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:45.170
Finally, all the stitches are back on the
bed and I'm ready to knit in tuck again,

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.950
not until after a good night's sleep.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.470
Lessons learned use lifelines.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:02.280
If I had left the waste yarn on after I
rehung the gathered skirt, or if I had

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:07.760
added a lifeline after creating that
pretty join and all this is is an e-wrap

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.459
join, I wouldn't be using
swear words right now.

00:03:12.899 --> 00:03:18.149
Lesson number two quit
before you get stupid tired.

00:03:19.709 --> 00:03:20.760
Nuff said.

00:03:21.705 --> 00:03:26.835
I hope you don't have to struggle like I
did, but if you do, maybe my experience

00:03:26.835 --> 00:03:27.585
will help you.

00:03:28.365 --> 00:03:29.385
Happy knitting.

